<exercisegroup cols="3" xml:id="exercisegroup-diagonalization-continue-calculate-all">
<title>Diagonalizing matrices</title>

<introduction><p>
	The following matrices have previously appeared in <xref ref="exercisegroup-eigen-values-vectors-calculate-all">Exercises</xref>,
	where you were previously asked to compute the characteristic polynomial (in factored form), eigenvalues, and eigenspaces for each matrix.
	Following up on that information,
	for each matrix:
	<ol>
		<li>
			Confirm <xref ref="corollary-diagonalization-evector-bases" /> by demonstrating that the full collection of calculated basis vectors from all the eigenspaces,
			taken all together,
			form a linearly independent set.
		</li>
		<li>
			Determine the <term><xref ref="definition-diagonalization-alg-mult" text="title" /></term>
			and <term><xref ref="definition-diagonalization-geom-mult" text="title" /></term>
			of each eigenvalue,
			and confirm that <xref ref="theorem-diagonalization-geom-alg-multiplicity" /> holds true in each case.
		</li>
		<li>
			State whether the matrix is diagonalizable and,
			if so,
			produce a transition matrix <m> P </m> and diagonal matrix <m> D </m> so that <m> \inv{P} A P = D </m> is true,
			where <m> A </m> is the given matrix.
			(<em>Note:</em> You should not need to calculate <m> \inv{P} </m> in order to produce <m> D </m>.)
		</li>
	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-2x2">
	<!-- 2×2, λ = -3 (Jordan 2×2) -->

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
			-1 \amp -4 \\
			 1 \amp -5
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>
		<li> A collection consisting of a single nonzero vector is always independent. </li>
		<li>
			The geometric multiplicity is <m> \dim E_{-3}(A) = 1 </m>
			and the algebraic multiplicity is <m> 2 </m>.
			This is consistent with <xref ref="theorem-diagonalization-geom-alg-multiplicity" /> as the geometric multiplicity is not greater than the algebraic multiplicity.
		</li>
		<li>
			This matrix is not diagonalizable because it has an eigenvalue whose geometric multiplicity is strictly less than its algebraic multiplicity.
		</li>
	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-1x1-J-1x1">
	<!-- 2×2, λ = 7, -2 -->

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
			-11 \amp 18 \\
			-9 \amp 16
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p>
		In these answers we use the eigenvectors from the provided answers to <xref ref="exercise-eigen-values-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-1x1-J-1x1" />,
		where needed.
		<ol>
			<li><p>
				Independence is verified by the calculation
				<md>
					\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
					\quad\rowredarrow\quad
					\bidentmattwo
				</md>.
			</p></li>
			<li>
				For each of the two eigenvalues both the geometric and algebraic multiplicities are equal to <m> 1 </m>.
				This is consistent with <xref ref="theorem-diagonalization-geom-alg-multiplicity" /> as in neither case is the geometric multiplicity is greater than the algebraic multiplicity.
			</li>
			<li><p>
				The matrix is diagonalizable,
				and a suitable transition matrix and corresponding diagonal form matrix are
				<md><mrow>
					P \amp = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix} \text{,}
					\amp
					D \amp = \begin{bmatrix} 7 \\ \amp -2 \end{bmatrix} \text{.}
				</mrow></md>
			</p></li>
		</ol>
	</p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-1x1-J-1x1-J-1x1">
	<!-- 3×3, λ = 6,1,4 -->

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
			 8 \amp - 4 \amp 0 \\
			 2 \amp   2 \amp 0 \\
			10 \amp -10 \amp 1
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p>
		In these answers we use the eigenvectors from the provided answers to <xref ref="exercise-eigen-values-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-1x1-J-1x1-J-1x1" />,
		where needed.
		<ol>
			<li><p>
				Independence is verified by the calculation
				<md>
					\begin{bmatrix}
						0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
						0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
						1 \amp 0 \amp 2
					\end{bmatrix}
					\quad\rowredarrow\quad
					\bidentmatthree
				</md>.
			</p></li>
			<li>
				For each of the three eigenvalues both the geometric and algebraic multiplicities are equal to <m> 1 </m>.
				This is consistent with <xref ref="theorem-diagonalization-geom-alg-multiplicity" /> as in no case is the geometric multiplicity is greater than the algebraic multiplicity.
			</li>
			<li><p>
				The matrix is diagonalizable,
				and a suitable transition matrix and corresponding diagonal form matrix are
				<md><mrow>
					P \amp =
					\begin{bmatrix}
						0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
						0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
						1 \amp 0 \amp 2
					\end{bmatrix}
					\text{,}
					\amp
					D \amp = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ \amp 4 \\ \amp \amp 6 \end{bmatrix}
					\text{.}
				</mrow></md>
			</p></li>
		</ol>
	</p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-S-J-1x1-1x1-J-1x1">
	<!-- 3×3, λ = -7,-7,0 -->

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
			-21 \amp  0 \amp   7 \\
			 28 \amp -7 \amp -14 \\
			-42 \amp  0 \amp  14
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p>
		In these answers we use the eigenvectors from the provided answers to <xref ref="exercise-eigen-values-vectors-calculate-all-S-J-1x1-1x1-J-1x1" />,
		where needed.
		<ol>
			<li><p>
				Independence is verified by the calculation
				<md>
					\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
						 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
						-2 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
						 3 \amp 2 \amp 0
					\end{abmatrix}
					\quad\rowredarrow\quad
					\bidentmatthree
				</md>.
			</p></li>
			<li>
				For eigenvalue <m> \lambda = 0 </m> both the geometric and algebraic multiplicities are <m> 1 </m>,
				and for eigenvalue <m> \lambda = -7 </m> both the geometric and algebraic multiplicities are <m> 2 </m>.
				This is consistent with <xref ref="theorem-diagonalization-geom-alg-multiplicity" /> as in neither case is the geometric multiplicity is greater than the algebraic multiplicity.
			</li>
			<li><p>
				The matrix is diagonalizable,
				and a suitable transition matrix and corresponding diagonal form matrix are
				<md><mrow>
					P \amp =
					\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
						 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
						-2 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
						 3 \amp 2 \amp 0
					\end{abmatrix}
					\text{,}
					\amp
					D \amp = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ \amp -7 \\ \amp \amp -7 \end{bmatrix}
					\text{.}
				</mrow></md>
			</p></li>
		</ol>
	</p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-2x2-J-1x1">
	<!-- 3×3, λ = -1 (Jordan 2×2), -3 -->

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
			 0 \amp -14 \amp -15 \\
			-1 \amp   7 \amp   9 \\
			 1 \amp -10 \amp -12
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p>
		In these answers we use the eigenvectors from the provided answers to <xref ref="exercise-eigen-values-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-2x2-J-1x1" />,
		where needed.
		<ol>
			<li><p>
				Independence is verified by the calculation
				<md>
					\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
						 1 \amp  6 \\
						-1 \amp -3 \\
						 1 \amp  4
					\end{abmatrix}
					\quad\rowredarrow\quad
					\bidentmatthree
				</md>.
			</p></li>
			<li>
				For eigenvalue <m> \lambda = -3 </m> both the geometric and algebraic multiplicities are <m> 1 </m>,
				but for eigenvalue <m> \lambda = -1 </m> the algebraic multiplicity is <m> 2 </m> while the geometric multiplicity is only <m> 1 </m>.
				This is consistent with <xref ref="theorem-diagonalization-geom-alg-multiplicity" /> as in neither case is the geometric multiplicity is greater than the algebraic multiplicity.
			</li>
			<li>
				This matrix is not diagonalizable because it has an eigenvalue whose geometric multiplicity is strictly less than its algebraic multiplicity.
			</li>
		</ol>
	</p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-2x2-1x1">
	<!-- 3×3, λ = 3 (Jordan 2×2,1×1) -->

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
			  0 \amp  3 \amp - 6 \\
			-13 \amp 16 \amp -26 \\
			- 5 \amp  5 \amp - 7
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>
		<li>
			Since there is only a single eigenvalue,
			we know that the basis vectors for that single eigenspace that we have already computed
			(see the provided answers to <xref ref="exercise-eigen-values-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-2x2-1x1" />)
			form an independent set.
		</li>
		<li>
			For the single eigenvalue the algebraic multiplicity is <m> 3 </m> while the geometric multiplicity is only <m> 2 </m>.
			This is consistent with <xref ref="theorem-diagonalization-geom-alg-multiplicity" /> as the geometric multiplicity is not greater than the algebraic multiplicity.
		</li>
		<li>
			This matrix is not diagonalizable because it has an eigenvalue whose geometric multiplicity is strictly less than its algebraic multiplicity.
		</li>
	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-S-J-3x3">
	<!-- 3×3, λ = 0 (Jordan 3×3) -->

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
			-1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
			-3 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
			-2 \amp 1 \amp 0
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>
		<li>
			Since there is only a single eigenvalue,
			we know that the single basis vector for that single eigenspace that we have already computed
			(see the provided answers to <xref ref="exercise-eigen-values-vectors-calculate-all-S-J-3x3" />)
			forms an independent set.
		</li>
		<li>
			For the single eigenvalue the algebraic multiplicity is <m> 3 </m> while the geometric multiplicity is only <m> 1 </m>.
			This is consistent with <xref ref="theorem-diagonalization-geom-alg-multiplicity" /> as the geometric multiplicity is not greater than the algebraic multiplicity.
		</li>
		<li>
			This matrix is not diagonalizable because it has an eigenvalue whose geometric multiplicity is strictly less than its algebraic multiplicity.
		</li>
	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-1x1-1x1-1x1-J-1x1">
	<!-- 4×4, λ = -4, -4, -4, -1 -->

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
			-19 \amp  0 \amp -30 \amp  0 \\
			  0 \amp -4 \amp   0 \amp  0 \\
			  9 \amp  0 \amp  14 \amp  0 \\
			- 3 \amp  0 \amp - 6 \amp -4
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p>
		In these answers we use the eigenvectors from the provided answers to <xref ref="exercise-eigen-values-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-1x1-1x1-1x1-J-1x1" />,
		where needed.
		<ol>
			<li><p>
				Independence is verified by the calculation
				<md>
					\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
						 5 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\
						 0 \amp 1 \amp  0 \amp 0 \\
						-3 \amp 0 \amp  1 \amp 0 \\
						 1 \amp 0 \amp  0 \amp 1
					\end{abmatrix}
					\quad\rowredarrow\quad
					\bidentmatfour
				</md>.
			</p></li>
			<li>
				For eigenvalue <m> \lambda = -1 </m> both the geometric and algebraic multiplicities are <m> 1 </m>,
				and for eigenvalue <m> \lambda = -4 </m> both the geometric and algebraic multiplicities are <m> 3 </m>.
				This is consistent with <xref ref="theorem-diagonalization-geom-alg-multiplicity" /> as in neither case is the geometric multiplicity is greater than the algebraic multiplicity.
			</li>
			<li><p>
				The matrix is diagonalizable,
				and a suitable transition matrix and corresponding diagonal form matrix are
				<md><mrow>
					P \amp =
					\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
						 5 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\
						 0 \amp 1 \amp  0 \amp 0 \\
						-3 \amp 0 \amp  1 \amp 0 \\
						 1 \amp 0 \amp  0 \amp 1
					\end{abmatrix}
					\text{,}
					\amp
 					D \amp = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ \amp -4 \\ \amp \amp -4 \\ \amp \amp \amp -4 \end{bmatrix}
					\text{.}
				</mrow></md>
			</p></li>
		</ol>
	</p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-S-J-2x2-J-1x1-J-1x1">
	<!-- 4×4, λ = -3 (Jordan 2×2), λ = 4, λ = 0 -->

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
			-19 \amp  5 \amp   1 \amp -18 \\
			-46 \amp  1 \amp -31 \amp -49 \\
			 18 \amp  1 \amp  17 \amp  19 \\
			  1 \amp -6 \amp -18 \amp - 1
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p>
		In these answers we use the eigenvectors from the provided answers to <xref ref="exercise-eigen-values-vectors-calculate-all-S-J-2x2-J-1x1-J-1x1" />,
		where needed.
		<ol>
			<li><p>
				Independence is verified by the calculation
				<md>
					\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
						- 8 \amp -1 \amp -2 \\
						-17 \amp -1 \amp -3 \\
						  5 \amp  0 \amp  1 \\
						  4 \amp  1 \amp  1
					\end{abmatrix}
					\quad\rowredarrow\quad
					\begin{bmatrix}
						1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
						0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
						0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
						0 \amp 0 \amp 0
					\end{bmatrix}
				</md>.
			</p></li>
			<li>
				For each of the eigenvalues <m> \lambda = 0 </m> and <m> \lambda = 4 </m> both the geometric and algebraic multiplicities are <m> 1 </m>,
				but for eigenvalue <m> \lambda = -3 </m> the algebraic multiplicity is <m> 2 </m> while the geometric multiplicity is only <m> 1 </m>.
				This is consistent with <xref ref="theorem-diagonalization-geom-alg-multiplicity" /> as in no case is the geometric multiplicity is greater than the algebraic multiplicity.
			</li>
			<li>
				This matrix is not diagonalizable because it has an eigenvalue whose geometric multiplicity is strictly less than its algebraic multiplicity.
			</li>
		</ol>
	</p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-2x2-1x1-J-1x1">
	<!-- 4×4, λ = -2 (Jordan 2×2), λ = -2, λ = 2 -->

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
			-4 \amp 34 \amp 4 \amp  36 \\
			 0 \amp 10 \amp 0 \amp  12 \\
			-1 \amp 23 \amp 0 \amp  24 \\
			 0 \amp -8 \amp 0 \amp -10
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p>
		In these answers we use the eigenvectors from the provided answers to <xref ref="exercise-eigen-values-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-2x2-1x1-J-1x1" />,
		where needed.
		<ol>
			<li><p>
				Independence is verified by the calculation
				<md>
					\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
						-9 \amp 2 \amp  1 \\
						-3 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
						-6 \amp 1 \amp  0 \\
						 2 \amp 0 \amp  1
					\end{abmatrix}
					\quad\rowredarrow\quad
					\begin{bmatrix}
						1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
						0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
						0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
						0 \amp 0 \amp 0
					\end{bmatrix}
				</md>.
			</p></li>
			<li>
				For eigenvalue <m> \lambda = 2 </m> both the geometric and algebraic multiplicities are <m> 1 </m>,
				but for eigenvalue <m> \lambda = -2 </m> the algebraic multiplicity is <m> 3 </m> while the geometric multiplicity is only <m> 2 </m>.
				This is consistent with <xref ref="theorem-diagonalization-geom-alg-multiplicity" /> as in neither case is the geometric multiplicity is greater than the algebraic multiplicity.
			</li>
			<li>
				This matrix is not diagonalizable because it has an eigenvalue whose geometric multiplicity is strictly less than its algebraic multiplicity.
			</li>
		</ol>
	</p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-3x3-J-1x1">
	<!-- 4×4, λ = 4 (Jordan 3×3), λ = -6 -->

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
			-1 \amp   5 \amp -1 \amp -1 \\
			 5 \amp - 1 \amp -1 \amp -1 \\
			28 \amp -27 \amp  3 \amp  0 \\
			25 \amp -25 \amp  1 \amp  5
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p>
		In these answers we use the eigenvectors from the provided answers to <xref ref="exercise-eigen-values-vectors-calculate-all-I-J-3x3-J-1x1" />,
		where needed.
		<ol>
			<li><p>
				The two basis eigenvectors are clearly independent from each other,
				as neither is a scalar multiple of the other.
			</p></li>
			<li>
				For eigenvalue <m> \lambda = 2-6 </m> both the geometric and algebraic multiplicities are <m> 1 </m>,
				but for eigenvalue <m> \lambda = 4 </m> the algebraic multiplicity is <m> 3 </m> while the geometric multiplicity is only <m> 1 </m>.
				This is consistent with <xref ref="theorem-diagonalization-geom-alg-multiplicity" /> as in neither case is the geometric multiplicity is greater than the algebraic multiplicity.
			</li>
			<li>
				This matrix is not diagonalizable because it has an eigenvalue whose geometric multiplicity is strictly less than its algebraic multiplicity.
			</li>
		</ol>
	</p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-vectors-calculate-all-S-J-4x4">
	<!-- 4×4, λ = 0 (Jordan 4×4) (nilpotent) -->

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
			 0 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp  1 \\
			-2 \amp  2 \amp -3 \amp -1 \\
			 1 \amp -1 \amp  1 \amp  1 \\
			-3 \amp  3 \amp -4 \amp -3
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>
		<li>
			Since there is only a single eigenvalue,
			we know that the single basis vector for that single eigenspace that we have already computed
			(see the provided answers to <xref ref="exercise-eigen-values-vectors-calculate-all-S-J-4x4" />)
			forms an independent set.
		</li>
		<li>
			For the single eigenvalue the algebraic multiplicity is <m> 4 </m> while the geometric multiplicity is only <m> 1 </m>.
			This is consistent with <xref ref="theorem-diagonalization-geom-alg-multiplicity" /> as the geometric multiplicity is not greater than the algebraic multiplicity.
		</li>
		<li>
			This matrix is not diagonalizable because it has an eigenvalue whose geometric multiplicity is strictly less than its algebraic multiplicity.
		</li>
	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
